--- THE ROOT CAUSE, MEASURED ---
The recurring failure was reproduced directly:

    apps/api/.env DATABASE_URL             : CONNECT OK  role=postgres
    apps/api/.env APP_SERVICE_DATABASE_URL : CONNECT FAILED
        InvalidPasswordError: password authentication failed for user
        "app_service"

The variable is SET but its password no longer authenticates. Every affected
verify script guards on presence, not connectivity:

    if APP_SERVICE_DATABASE_URL:
        conn = await _connect(APP_SERVICE_DATABASE_URL)

The guard passes; the connection fails; callers fall back to
`SET LOCAL ROLE app_service`. So an RLS test intended to prove "a non-bypass
role cannot read across orgs" instead proved "a postgres connection wearing a
role hat cannot" — strictly weaker, reported green. Four consecutive sprints.

This is the general lesson, and it recurs: a presence check is not a capability
check. `if VAR:` proves a string is non-empty and nothing else.

